<v SPEAKER_00>Thank you very, very much for the question. There's a lot of discussion internally about what are the best ways to not only build, but materials to use, means and methods, which are technically not the responsibility of the designer. But we talk about that a lot because materials and methods go to what it's going to cost to maintain or operate the facility. sometimes that means looking at equipment that is obsolete the factor machine for instance is is one where which has a useful life of 20 years if that's an extraordinary amount of time for this kind of equipment but it's necessary to be able to cost-effectively maintain the infrastructure that we're talking about in other cases we look at the location of the infrastructure to be built, what properties need easements, what properties need to be acquired, or what kinds of construction methods are required to build that particular work. So there's a lot of often cost-benefit analysis in the nature of the early discussions. Our consultants are asked constantly, is this the best way to do this? What are not only the capital cost implications, But the operations cost implications. Are we going to have to hire more people to take care of this particular infrastructure in this particular way? Or can we maintain it basically with the current staff but with different equipment? Sometimes we're making a decision about technology. Is it technology that will not only maintain or improve the water quality, but is it technology that will reduce the cost maintaining that there's a as a municipal run utility there's a lot of that kind of balancing act that we're that we often are doing because we don't have the ability to go for the hottest most expensive shiniest often most efficient way of doing it we don't have the ability to raise the rates at the levels that the private private utilities do and yet we are required and and are very proud of the fact that we meet the same standards that all the other utilities do. So these are constant discussions. Almost every one of our discussions, especially with our consultants and when we're talking about the sewer fund, we're also sharing these costs with several outside municipalities. And so they, too, query us about, is this the best way to do it, the most effective way to do it, the most efficient way to do it, the best equipment to do it? To what extent do they have to be part of that solution to what extent do can we reduce or delay or phase the work in a different way that's always an integral part of the discussion thank

